      "question": [
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "259213",
          "text": "<p>Below function is defined from positive integers to all integers</p>\n\n<p>$f(n) = \\begin{cases} (n-1)/2 &amp; \\text{if n is odd} \\\\ -n/2 &amp; \\text{if n is even} \\end{cases}$</p>\n\n<p>Which of the following statements regarding $f$&nbsp;is TRUE?</p>\n\n<ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n<li>It is one to one but not onto</li>\n<li>It is onto but not one to one</li>\n<li>It is bijection</li>\n<li>It is neither one-one nor&nbsp;onto</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "c",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "gb2019-dm3,functions",
          "category": "Set Theory & Algebra"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "259212",
          "text": "<p><img alt=\"\" height=\"143\" src=\"http://thegatebook.in/qa/?qa=blob&amp;qa_blobid=259845039847183625\" width=\"600\"></p>\n\n<p>Let $A, C \\subseteq R^2$ where $A=\\{(x, y)/y=2x+1\\}$, and $C=\\{(x,y)/x-y=7 \\}$. The number of elements in $\\overline{\\bar{A} \\cup \\bar{C}}$ is</p>\n\n<ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>$1$</li>\n    <li>$2$</li>\n    <li>$\\text{Infinitely many}$</li>\n    <li>$\\text{Uncountably&nbsp;many}$</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "a",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "gb2019-dm3",
          "category": "Set Theory & Algebra"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "259211",
          "text": "<p><img alt=\"\" height=\"44\" src=\"http://thegatebook.in/qa/?qa=blob&amp;qa_blobid=18242536141443663885\" width=\"594\"></p>\n\n<p>If $A=\\{1, 2, 3, 4, 5\\}$ and there are $6720$ Injective functions $f: A \\rightarrow B$ then $\\mid B \\mid =$ ______?</p>",
          "type": "Numerical",
          "answer": "8",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "gb2019-dm3,numerical-answers,functions",
          "category": "Set Theory & Algebra"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "259207",
          "text": "<p><img alt=\"\" height=\"141\" src=\"http://thegatebook.in/qa/?qa=blob&amp;qa_blobid=12433427477059005196\" width=\"545\"></p>\n\n<p>How many functions from $A=\\{1, 2, 3, 4, 5\\}$ to $B=\\{1, 2, 3, 4, 5\\}$ are invertible? Note: A function is invertible if it has an inverse function</p>\n\n<ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>$120$</li>\n    <li>$3125$</li>\n    <li>$125$</li>\n    <li>$25$</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "a",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "gb2019-dm3",
          "category": "Set Theory & Algebra"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "259204",
          "text": "<p><img alt=\"\" height=\"145\" src=\"http://thegatebook.in/qa/?qa=blob&amp;qa_blobid=2204912884091534917\" width=\"497\"></p>\n\n<p>Let $A = \\{1, 2, 3, 4\\}$. Consider the following statements about relations on set$A$. Which of the following statements is false?</p>\n\n<ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>every relation $R$ on $A$ with $15$ ordered pair members is not transitive</li>\n    <li>Intersection of any two transitive relations is transitive</li>\n    <li>Union if any two symmetric relations is symmetric</li>\n    <li>Complement of a symmetric relation might not be symmetric</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "d",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "gb2019-dm3",
          "category": "Set Theory & Algebra"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "259203",
          "text": "<p><img alt=\"\" height=\"154\" src=\"http://thegatebook.in/qa/?qa=blob&amp;qa_blobid=3094561461803892441\" width=\"600\"></p>\n\n<p>Given a simple undirected graph $G$. Define the relation $R(u, v)$ between nodes $u$ and $v$ of $G$ as follows:</p>\n\n<p>$R(u, v) = \\text{ there is a path from u to v in G}$</p>\n\n<p>Which one of the following statements is true?</p>\n\n<ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>$R$ is reflexive but not symmetric</li>\n    <li>$R$ is symmetric but not reflexive</li>\n    <li>$R$ is an Equivalence relation</li>\n    <li>$R$ is Partial order relation</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "c",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "gb2019-dm3",
          "category": "Set Theory & Algebra"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "259200",
          "text": "The number of equivalence relations on a set of $3$ elements is _______",
          "type": "Numerical",
          "answer": "5",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "gb2019-dm3,numerical-answers,relations",
          "category": "Set Theory & Algebra"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "259198",
          "text": "<p><img alt=\"\" height=\"109\" src=\"http://thegatebook.in/qa/?qa=blob&amp;qa_blobid=9504082444874852098\" width=\"600\"></p>\n\n<p>Which of the following statements is true about posets which are defined on infinte elements?</p>\n\n<ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>There cannot be maximal element</li>\n    <li>There cannot be minimal element</li>\n    <li>Always there is a minimal element</li>\n    <li>None of the above</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "d",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "gb2019-dm3",
          "category": "Set Theory & Algebra"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "259196",
          "text": "<p><img alt=\"\" height=\"368\" src=\"http://thegatebook.in/qa/?qa=blob&amp;qa_blobid=3417115558680941406\" width=\"359\"></p>\n\n<p><img alt=\"\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\"></p>\n\n<p>Which of the following is true about above diagram?</p>\n\n<ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>It is lattice but not complemented</li>\n    <li>It is not lattice</li>\n    <li>It is complemented lattice</li>\n    <li>It is not meet semilattice</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "a",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "gb2019-dm3",
          "category": "Set Theory & Algebra"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "259195",
          "text": "<p><img alt=\"\" height=\"127\" src=\"http://thegatebook.in/qa/?qa=blob&amp;qa_blobid=18166398709778790197\" width=\"534\"></p>\n\n<p>Given that $A=\\{1, 2, 3, 4\\}$ and $R$ is the relation efined by $(x,y) \\in R$ if $3x+2y \\leq 11$. Which of the following statements is true?</p>\n\n<ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>It is reflexive</li>\n    <li>It is symmetric</li>\n    <li>It is trasnitive</li>\n    <li>None</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "d",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "gb2019-dm3",
          "category": "Set Theory & Algebra"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "259210",
          "text": "For $n \\in Z^+$ define $X_n=\\{1, 2, 3, 4, \\dots , n\\}$. Given $m, n \\in Z^+, \\: f:X_m \\rightarrow X_n$ is called monotonically increasing if for all $i, j \\in X_m \\: 1 \\leq i < j \\leq m \\Rightarrow f(i) \\leq f(j)$. How many monotonically increasing functions are there with domain $X_7$ and co domain $X_5$?",
          "type": "Numerical",
          "answer": "330",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "gb2019-dm3,functions,numerical-answers",
          "category": "Set Theory & Algebra"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "259209",
          "text": "<p><img alt=\"\" height=\"130\" src=\"http://thegatebook.in/qa/?qa=blob&amp;qa_blobid=15335130819311682504\" width=\"574\"></p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>For $A=\\{1, 2, 3, 4, \\dots 7\\}$. How many bijective functions $f: A \\rightarrow A$ satisfy $f(1) \\neq 1$?</p>\n\n<ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>$7! -6!$</li>\n    <li>$6!$</li>\n    <li>$7.6!$</li>\n    <li>$\\text{none}$</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "a",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "gb2019-dm3",
          "category": "Set Theory & Algebra"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "259208",
          "text": "<p><img alt=\"\" height=\"59\" src=\"http://thegatebook.in/qa/?qa=blob&amp;qa_blobid=2424431598375747702\" width=\"439\"></p>\n\n<p>Let $f, g: R \\rightarrow R$ where $g(x)=1-x+x^2$ and $f(x)=ax+b$ . If $(g \\: o \\: f)(x)=9x^2-9x+3$ then determine $a=$__________</p>",
          "type": "Numerical",
          "answer": "3",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "gb2019-dm3,numerical-answers,functions",
          "category": "Set Theory & Algebra"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "259206",
          "text": "<p><img alt=\"\" height=\"118\" src=\"http://thegatebook.in/qa/?qa=blob&amp;qa_blobid=12625683445026679852\" width=\"529\"></p>\n\n<p>Number of onto functions from set of $n$ elements $(n&gt;2)$ to set of $3$ elements?</p>\n\n<ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>$3^n-3$</li>\n    <li>$3^n-3\\times2^n-3$</li>\n    <li>$3^n-3\\times2^n+3$</li>\n    <li>$3^n-2^n-13$</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "c",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "gb2019-dm3",
          "category": "Set Theory & Algebra"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "259205",
          "text": "<p><img alt=\"\" height=\"216\" src=\"http://thegatebook.in/qa/?qa=blob&amp;qa_blobid=7192795496020457981\" width=\"594\"></p>\n\n<p>Let $A$ and $B$ be two&nbsp;sets such that $\\mid A \\mid =\\mid B \\mid$ and there is a one to one function, $f$, from $A$ to $B$. Which of the following must be true about $f$?</p>\n\n<p>S1: $f$ is onto function</p>\n\n<p>S2: $f$ has an inverse</p>\n\n<ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n<li>Only S1</li>\n<li>Only S2</li>\n<li>Both S1 and S2</li>\n<li>None of the above</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "c",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "gb2019-dm3,functions",
          "category": "Set Theory & Algebra"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "259201",
          "text": "Let $A$ be a set with $\\mid A \\mid =5$. How many antisymmetric relations are possible on set $A$ whose size is maximum?",
          "type": "Numerical",
          "answer": "1024",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "gb2019-dm3,numerical-answers,relations",
          "category": "Set Theory & Algebra"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "259199",
          "text": "Let $A = \\{1,2,3,4,5,6,7\\}.$ The number of symmetric relations on $A$ which contains exactly $4$ elements is _______",
          "type": "Numerical",
          "answer": "686",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "gb2019-dm3,relations,numerical-answers",
          "category": "Set Theory & Algebra"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "259197",
          "text": "<p><img alt=\"\" height=\"126\" src=\"http://thegatebook.in/qa/?qa=blob&amp;qa_blobid=8213420132804972284\" width=\"600\"></p>\n\n<p>Let $R$ denote a relation on the set of all ordered pairs of positive integers by $(x,y)R(u,v)$ if and only if $xv=yu$. Which of the following is true?</p>\n\n<ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>$R$ is a partial order relation</li>\n    <li>$R$ is an&nbsp; equivalence relation</li>\n    <li>$R$ is neither partial order relation nor equivalence&nbsp;relation</li>\n    <li>$R$ is an antishymmetric&nbsp;relation</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "b",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "gb2019-dm3",
          "category": "Set Theory & Algebra"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "259194",
          "text": "<p><img alt=\"\" height=\"293\" src=\"http://thegatebook.in/qa/?qa=blob&amp;qa_blobid=12146589967783456560\" width=\"600\"></p>\n\n<p>A partition P1 is called a refinement of the partition P2 if every set in P1 is a subset of one of the sets in P2.</p>\n\n<p>S1: The partition of te set of bit strings of length $16$ formed by equivalence classes of bit strings that agree on the last eight bits is a refinement of the partition formed from the equivalence classes of bit strings that agree on the last four bits.</p>\n\n<p>S2: The partition formed from congruence classes modulo $6$ is a refinement of the partition formed from congruence classes modulo $3$.</p>\n\n<p>Which of the following statements is true?</p>\n\n<ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>Only S1</li>\n    <li>Only S2</li>\n    <li>Both S1 and S2</li>\n    <li>None of the above</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "c",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "gb2019-dm3",
          "category": "Set Theory & Algebra"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "259193",
          "text": "<p><img alt=\"\" height=\"226\" src=\"http://thegatebook.in/qa/?qa=blob&amp;qa_blobid=14765340273690963995\" width=\"600\"></p>\n\n<p>Suppose that $R!$ and $R2$ are two equivalence relations on non empty domain $A$ then which of the following statements is true?</p>\n\n<ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type:upper-roman\">\n    <li>$R1 \\cup R2$ is an equivalence relation</li>\n    <li>$R1 \\cap R2$ is an equivalence relation</li>\n    <li>$R1 \\oplus&nbsp;R2$ is an equivalence relation</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p>Which of the following statements is true?</p>\n\n<ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>Only I</li>\n    <li>Only&nbsp;II</li>\n    <li>Only Ii and III</li>\n    <li>Only III</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "b",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "gb2019-dm3",
          "category": "Set Theory & Algebra"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}